{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Multimedia Storyboarding and AI Asset Generation", "title": "Image Generation for Instruction", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting image generation for instruction to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation", "ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "03", "section": "03", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/03/02"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_calibration"]}, "content": "# Image Generation for Instruction\n\n![Image Generation for Instruction textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/03-03-image-generation-for-instruction.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nGenerative image tools have collapsed the cost of producing a striking picture to near zero, which is exactly why they are dangerous in instruction. A text-to-image model optimizes for plausibility and aesthetic appeal, not for instructional accuracy, and it has no model of your learning objective. It will happily render a \"lever\" that violates physics, a \"cell\" with the wrong organelles, or a richly detailed scene whose detail is precisely the seductive distraction that depresses learning. The designer's job shifts from making images to commissioning, screening, and verifying them.\n\nThis section treats image generation as a specification-and-verification problem. The instructional value of an image is set before the prompt, by naming the function the image must perform (what relationship or distinction the learner must be able to read from it), and confirmed after generation by checking the render against the content, not against your taste. A beautiful image that is decorative, inaccurate, or detail-laden is not an asset; it is a liability the tool produced cheaply.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer needs an image of a plant cell for a middle-school biology frame. The first generation is gorgeous: lush green, dramatic lighting, a cell bursting with detail. It is also wrong. The model has rendered animal-cell features inside a structure labeled \"plant cell,\" added several organelles the lesson never covers, and buried the cell wall, the one structure the frame is supposed to teach, behind decorative texture. Accepted on looks, this image would teach a misconception.\n\nThe picture's appeal is the trap. Because it looks authoritative and was free to make, the designer is tempted to ship it. This section asks for the opposite reflex: a generated image is a draft to be interrogated against the content and the objective before it earns a place on the frame.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn instructional image has a **representational function**: it makes a relationship, structure, or distinction visible so the learner can read it directly rather than inferring it from text. Specifying that function is step one, and it constrains everything downstream. \"Show a lever\" is not a function; \"show that moving the fulcrum toward the load increases mechanical advantage\" is, because it tells you the image must contrast two effort-arm lengths, which a single pretty lever cannot do.\n\nTwo principles govern what to keep out. Mayer's **coherence principle** says removing interesting-but-extraneous material improves learning; the corollary is the **seductive-detail effect**, where vivid, engaging elements (the photorealistic studio, the dramatic light) actively pull attention from the to-be-learned relationship. Generative tools manufacture seductive detail by default, so coherence is usually a subtraction task: re-prompt toward a clean diagram, not a rich scene.\n\nTwo more checks decide whether the image is fit to ship:\n\n- **Accuracy verification.** The model has no commitment to truth, so every label, count, proportion, and relationship must be checked against the authoritative content source. Treat the first render as an unverified claim.\n- **UDL and access.** An image that carries meaning by color alone, or that has no functional alt text, excludes learners; representational images create an accessibility obligation handled in 03.05.\n\nCognitive load theory ties these together: extraneous visual detail and inaccurate elements both spend working memory the learner needs for the actual relationship, so a \"better-looking\" image can be a worse instructional one.\n\n## Worked Example: Choosing and Verifying a Generated Instructional Image\n\nThe lesson teaches how a lever multiplies force. Below is the before/after of one image decision.\n\n**Before (first generated image, accepted on looks):**\n\n> Prompt: photorealistic woodworking studio, dramatic light, a crowbar resting on a workbench among many tools. The render is striking. Caption: looks great, students will love it.\n\nProblems: the scene is decorative. The fulcrum, load, and effort are not distinguishable, the crowbar sits among competing detail (seductive detail), and nothing was verified against the lever model.\n\n**After (re-prompted toward the learning function, then verified):**\n\n> Learning function named first: the learner must see that moving the fulcrum closer to the load increases mechanical advantage. Prompt: clean labeled line diagram, one lever, fulcrum-load-effort marked, two effort-arm lengths shown side by side. Caption: shows the relationship, then I checked it.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Started from the learning function (fulcrum position changes advantage), not from a style prompt.\n2. Cut decorative and seductive detail: removed the studio scene so attention stays on the lever geometry.\n3. Verified accuracy: confirmed the diagram labels effort, load, and fulcrum correctly and that the longer effort arm is drawn on the higher-advantage side. The first generated draft had swapped load and effort labels; that was corrected.\n4. Rejected suggestion: the AI proposed adding a person pushing the lever for relatability. Rejected, because the figure added detail without clarifying the distance relationship the lesson depends on.\n\nThe improvement is not a prettier image. It is an image chosen for its instructional function and verified against the content.\n\nWork the screening of the first lever render one gate at a time, predicting each verdict before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Screening the first generated lever image\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The first render is a photorealistic studio with a crowbar among many tools. Apply the function gate: can a learner read the rule that moving the fulcrum toward the load increases mechanical advantage from this image?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, a crowbar is clearly a lever\",\"No, a single lever cannot show two effort-arm lengths, so the relationship is invisible\",\"Yes, the caption explains it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The function requires contrasting two effort-arm lengths. One pretty lever cannot show a comparison, so the relationship is not readable and the image fails the function gate. You do not patch a failed function gate with caption text.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Suppose you re-prompt to a clean diagram that does show the relationship. Apply the coherence gate: what should you strip?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The labels for fulcrum, load, and effort\",\"The studio scenery, dramatic lighting, and any extra figures\",\"The two effort-arm lengths\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Strip the seductive detail (scenery, lighting, added people) that competes with the geometry. The labels and the two arm lengths are the function and must stay.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The accuracy gate then catches what the eye glides past: the first diagram had swapped the load and effort labels. One verified error means the image is not shippable until corrected. Polish is the last gate, never the first.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nCommission, then verify, in that order. Before prompting, write the image's function in one sentence: \"the learner must be able to read [relationship/structure/distinction] from this image.\" The prompt then specifies that function (clean diagram, labeled parts, two cases shown side by side) rather than a mood.\n\nAfter generation, run the three-gate screen and reject at the first failure:\n\n1. **Function gate:** Can a learner read the named relationship from this image without reading the caption? If the relationship is not visible, re-prompt; do not patch it with text.\n2. **Coherence gate:** What in this render is decorative or seductive (scenery, lighting, extra figures, ornamental detail)? Strip it; if the model will not strip it, switch to a diagram style.\n3. **Accuracy gate:** Check every label, count, and proportion against the source. Models routinely swap labels (load/effort), miscount items, and invent parts. One verified error means the image is not shippable until corrected.\n\nPolish is the last gate, not the first; a striking image that fails any earlier gate is rejected regardless of how good it looks.\n\nPut the commission-then-verify workflow in the order a defensible image decision follows.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Write the image function in one sentence before prompting\",\"Prompt for that function (clean diagram, labeled parts, side-by-side cases) not a mood\",\"Function gate: confirm the relationship is readable without the caption\",\"Coherence gate: strip decorative and seductive detail\",\"Accuracy gate: verify every label, count, and proportion against the source\",\"Polish: refine style only after the earlier gates pass\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"photorealism equals quality.\" For most instructional functions a clean line diagram outperforms a photo, because the photo carries surface detail (texture, background, lighting) that competes with the relationship being taught. The second misreading is trusting the model on facts: because the render looks confident and professional, designers skip verification, and ship images with swapped labels or impossible structures. The model is fluent, not accurate; fluency is exactly what makes its errors hard to catch.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A generated 'plant cell' image is gorgeous but renders animal-cell organelles and buries the cell wall under decorative texture. Which gate catches this, and why is its appeal a trap?\" explanation=\"The accuracy gate catches it: the model has no commitment to truth, so its confident, professional render is an unverified claim with wrong structures. The polish that makes it tempting is exactly what hides the error, so appeal must never substitute for verification against the content source.\" options='[{\"text\":\"No gate, because the image looks authoritative and was free to make\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The accuracy gate, because the render is an unverified claim and its polish hides the structural errors\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The polish gate, because the texture should be smoother\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a real commission. A frame must teach that series circuits share one current path while parallel circuits split it. Function sentence: \"the learner must be able to read, from a side-by-side image, that current has one path in series and multiple paths in parallel.\" First prompt yields a photorealistic breadboard with tangled jumper wires, beautiful and useless, because the single-versus-multiple-path distinction is invisible under the clutter (function gate fails). Re-prompt: \"schematic, two simple circuits side by side, left labeled series with one loop, right labeled parallel with two branches, no breadboard, no photoreal detail.\" The second render shows the contrast cleanly (function gate passes, coherence gate passes). Accuracy gate: confirm the parallel branches actually rejoin and the battery polarity is consistent; the first draft had drawn the branches without a shared node, which would teach a wrong model, so that is corrected before shipping.\n\nThe walkthrough shows the order doing the work: naming the function ruled out the photoreal style, coherence ruled out the clutter, and verification caught a structural error the eye glides past.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every generated image as an unverified claim and audit it. For each image in the chapter, name the function it is supposed to perform, then list what in the render is extraneous to that function and what you would check against a source. A strong reader does not ask \"is this image good?\" but \"what relationship must a learner read here, is it readable, and is it true?\" Train the habit of finding the one decorative element that, if removed, would lose nothing instructional, and the one factual claim the picture makes that you would not yet trust.\n\nA strong reader of instructional image generation can, for any image, state its representational function, point to the seductive detail to cut, and name the specific fact to verify.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when the trace shows the commission-and-verification cycle, not just a final picture. It records the function sentence written before prompting, at least one rejected generation with the gate it failed (decorative, inaccurate, or wrong style), and an explicit verification note (\"checked fulcrum, load, effort labels against the physics text; corrected a swapped pair\"). Where an AI suggestion added relatable detail (a person, a setting), the trace records rejecting it for failing the coherence gate.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"I re-prompted the lever image away from a photoreal workshop toward a labeled diagram that contrasts two effort-arm lengths, because the original could not show the distance relationship the lesson depends on. I verified the effort, load, and fulcrum labels against the source and corrected a swapped pair. I rejected the tool's suggestion to add a person pushing the lever because it added detail without clarifying the relationship.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove from a generated diagram to a generated photo for a soft-skills scenario, say a customer-service role-play image. The accuracy gate changes character: there is no physics to check, but there is representation to check, because the model defaults to stereotyped depictions of role, gender, age, and ethnicity that can misteach or alienate. The coherence and function gates still apply (does the image show the interpersonal dynamic the scenario teaches, free of distracting detail?), but verification now means auditing for bias and representativeness against the actual learner population. The transfer test is whether you can keep the commission-then-verify discipline when \"accuracy\" means social accuracy rather than factual accuracy. If your verification step only knows how to catch a swapped label, it will pass an image that teaches a stereotype.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-03-image-generation-for-instruction-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.03. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect image generation for instruction to the evidence-based revision that verifies it and the bounded AI support that produced the draft.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction", "stem": "You can ship one of two generated images for a lesson on how a lever multiplies force: a photorealistic workshop scene with a lever among many tools, or a plain line diagram labeling the fulcrum, load, and effort distances. Which better serves instruction, and why?", "options": ["The photorealistic scene, because realism always increases motivation.", "The line diagram, because it isolates the fulcrum-load-effort relationship the learner must grasp, without competing detail.", "The photorealistic scene, because it has more pixels and looks professional.", "Either one, since image choice does not affect learning."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Instructional value comes from representing the target relationship clearly. Photorealistic detail here is extraneous and can crowd out the lever mechanics the learner needs to see.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_realism_equals_instructional", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction", "stem": "A designer adds a vivid generated image of a lightning storm to a slide about static electricity, mainly because it looks dramatic. What is the design risk?", "options": ["The image file will be too large to load.", "The dramatic but tangential detail can act as seductive detail, diverting attention from the charge-transfer point being taught.", "Lightning images are copyrighted by default.", "Storms cannot be generated accurately by any model."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An engaging but off-topic image competes with the essential content. If the image does not carry the learning point, it adds extraneous processing.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_decorative_image_is_neutral", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction", "stem": "An AI tool generates a labeled diagram of the human heart for your module. Before placing it, what is the essential check that keeps you the designer of record?", "options": ["Confirm the image resolution is at least 1080p.", "Verify every chamber label, vessel, and flow arrow against an anatomy source, because generated diagrams can render confident but wrong content.", "Check that the diagram matches the slide's color theme.", "Trust the diagram, since the model was trained on medical data."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Generated images can look authoritative while mislabeling or miscounting parts. Verifying against the content source is the bounded, non-delegable check.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_unverified_generated_content", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_image_generation_for_instruction_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction", "stem": "A learner spends an hour refining an image prompt for lighting, camera angle, and art style, then asks why reviewers still call the image instructionally weak. What did the prompt work most likely skip?", "options": ["A higher output resolution setting.", "Stating the learning function the image must serve, so the visual choices target what the learner needs to understand.", "A more popular art style.", "A watermark for attribution."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Prompt craft for style does not substitute for design judgment. Without naming the learning function first, polished prompting still yields decoration.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_prompt_is_the_artifact", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_image_generation_for_instruction"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "f1e8258b06e058a47cc445c9f25388e63024245537ca1c86d077f4916364c1f0", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_realism_equals_instructional", "pattern": "photorealism_as_instructional_value", "description": "Judging a generated image by how realistic or detailed it looks rather than by whether it represents the to-be-learned relationship clearly", "trigger": "learner prefers a richly rendered scene over a simpler diagram that shows the target relationship", "feedback": "Ask what the image must teach. Decorative detail can add extraneous load. The best instructional image shows the relationship, not the most pixels.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_decorative_image_is_neutral", "pattern": "decorative_image_assumed_harmless", "description": "Assuming an unrelated or seductive generated image is harmless because it looks engaging", "trigger": "learner adds a vivid but off-topic image to make the screen lively", "feedback": "Seductive detail can divert attention from the essential. If the image does not carry the learning point, it competes with it.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_unverified_generated_content", "pattern": "generated_image_accuracy_unchecked", "description": "Treating a generated instructional image as factually correct without checking it against the content source", "trigger": "learner ships a generated diagram with miscounted parts or wrong labels because it looked authoritative", "feedback": "Generated images can render confident but wrong content. Verify every label, count, and relationship against the source before use.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_3_3_prompt_is_the_artifact", "pattern": "prompt_quality_mistaken_for_design", "description": "Believing a clever prompt alone makes the image instructionally sound, skipping the design judgment about what the learner needs", "trigger":